    "q-127310": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127310",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{2y}+4x,\\,{x}^{7}-{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,-1\\le y\\le 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14235132/assets/09acd3df62b333bdaeb7553599507d4dbad7412ef2770b00e8fc8855b38a774e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{2y}+4x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}-{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)[x]_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[4y-{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(4(-1)-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
