    "q-127326": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127326",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14235132/assets/f7400787e09600d370fa5c554e616fad6ba3a126a09725cfb236502f81a7e70e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4xy-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(-4(1)^{2}y-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4y-6{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[-2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3})-(-2(-1)^{2}-2(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
